<!--
    structsynth: two functions that read overlapping subsets of one record get
    ONE synthesized structure, not one each.

    Source shape (hand-assembled, no prologue, so each parameter arrives in RDI
    with nothing declaring its type):

        int three_fields(void *p) {              // @0x100000
            unsigned char *s = *(unsigned char **)p;   // offset 0,  8 bytes
            int n = *(int *)((char *)p + 8);           // offset 8,  4 bytes
            n += *(int *)((char *)p + 0xc);            // offset 0xc, 4 bytes
            return n + *s;
        }
        int two_fields(void *p) {                // @0x100010
            unsigned char *s = *(unsigned char **)p;   // offset 0, 8 bytes
            int n = *(int *)((char *)p + 8);           // offset 8, 4 bytes
            return n + *s;
        }

        three_fields                    two_fields
        48 8b 07  mov rax,[rdi]         48 8b 07  mov rax,[rdi]
        8b 4f 08  mov ecx,[rdi+8]       8b 4f 08  mov ecx,[rdi+8]
        03 4f 0c  add ecx,[rdi+0xc]     0f b6 00  movzx eax,byte [rax]
        0f b6 00  movzx eax,byte [rax]  01 c8     add eax,ecx
        01 c8     add eax,ecx           c3        ret
        c3        ret

    `two_fields` claims offsets {0, 8}; `three_fields` claims {0, 8, 0xc} of the
    same record. Comparing layouts for equality gives them different names.

    Default (assert #1): both bodies are raw offset arithmetic over an untyped
    pointee.

    With `option structsynth param` (asserts 2 to 5): `three_fields` mints the
    layout, and `two_fields`, whose claims it contains at the same offsets with
    the same widths and types, is answered with the SAME `struct_0` rather than
    a name of its own.

    The second pair is the shape that must NOT merge: two integer words are how
    distinct records begin (`struct stat` and gnulib's `cycle_check_state` both
    start `{dev, ino}`), so a reader that measured only those is answered by
    its own shape, never by a larger record that happens to begin the same way.

        long four_words(void *p) {       // @0x100020: {0,8,0x10: 8 bytes; 0x18: 4}
            return *(long *)p + *(long *)((char *)p + 8)
                 + *(long *)((char *)p + 0x10) + *(unsigned *)((char *)p + 0x18);
        }
        long two_words(void *p) {        // @0x100040: {0, 8}
            return *(long *)p + *(long *)((char *)p + 8);
        }

        48 8b 07     mov rax,[rdi]          48 8b 07     mov rax,[rdi]
        48 03 47 08  add rax,[rdi+8]        48 03 47 08  add rax,[rdi+8]
        48 03 47 10  add rax,[rdi+0x10]     c3           ret
        8b 57 18     mov edx,[rdi+0x18]
        48 01 d0     add rax,rdx
        c3           ret

    Asserts 6 and 7: `four_words` mints `struct_1`, and `two_words` mints
    `struct_2` instead of being answered with it.

    The third pair is an interface table, the shape of rsyslogd's
    `*QueryInterface` functions: a version word at offset 0 and then nothing but
    function pointers. A `code *` names no record, so distinct tables differ
    only in how many slots they have, and a table is answered only by its own
    shape. Without that rule rsyslogd's seven interface records (80 to 136
    bytes) all took the 144-byte layout of the largest.

        int big_if(struct if5 *p) {      // @0x100060: {0: 4 bytes; 8..0x28: code *}
            p->ver = 0xd; p->f[0..4] = fn_0 .. fn_4; return 0;
        }
        int small_if(struct if3 *p) {    // @0x1000a0: {0: 4 bytes; 8..0x18: code *}
            p->ver = 1; p->f[0..2] = fn_5 .. fn_7; return 0;
        }

        c7 07 0d 00 00 00           mov dword [rdi], 0xd
        48 c7 47 08 00 01 10 00     mov qword [rdi+8], fn_0 (0x100100)
        ... one store per slot, fn_N at 0x100100 + 4*N ...
        31 c0                       xor eax, eax
        c3                          ret

    Asserts 8 and 9: `big_if`, decompiled first, mints `struct_3`, and
    `small_if`, whose claims it contains, still mints `struct_4`, so two
    definitions declare slot 0x18.
-->
